<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Altan Khan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1507–<a href="/years/1582">1582</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Altan Khan was leader of the Tümed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a>, a grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dayan-khan">Dayan Khan</a> who raided Ming <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> for decades. In 1571 he made peace with the Ming, gaining trade and a royal title, and he founded the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hohhot">Hohhot</a>. In 1578 he met the Tibetan lama <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sonam-gyatso">Sonam Gyatso</a> and gave him the title <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalai-lama">Dalai Lama</a>, beginning the conversion of the Mongols to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gelug">Gelug Buddhism</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/altan-khan.jpg" alt="Altan Khan" width="150" height="232" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Altan_Khan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Altan was the second son of Bars Bolud Jinong and a grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dayan-khan">Dayan Khan</a>. After Dayan Khan the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> formed six tümen divided into a Left Wing under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-khan">Great Khan</a> and a Right Wing under Altan's father, who held the office of jinong, or vice-regent. Altan ruled the Tümed, whose pastures lay north of the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanxi">Shanxi</a>. When his elder brother Gün Bilig died in 1542, Altan became the effective leader of the Right Wing. After the death of Bodi Alagh Khan in 1547 he forced the new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khagan">khagan</a>, Darayisung Küdeng Khan, to flee eastward, and in 1551 Darayisung compromised by granting him the title <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gegeen-khan">Gegeen Khan</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Altan wanted to trade horses for Chinese farm produce and textiles, but the Ming court, suspicious of him, repeatedly refused and sometimes killed his envoys. He raided northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> in 1529, 1530 and 1542, and in 1550 crossed gaps in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-wall-of-china">Great Wall</a> and besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>, setting its suburbs on fire. He strengthened his Tümed base by settling Chinese refugees there and kept a network of Ming conspirators led by Zhao Quan, a disciple of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/white-lotus">White Lotus</a>. In 1552 he gained control of the remains of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karakorum">Karakorum</a>.</p><p>In 1570 his grandson Daičing Ejei Taiji defected to the Ming after a family quarrel. Ministers of the Longqing Emperor proposed that Altan accept a Ming title and hand over Zhao Quan in return for his grandson and trading rights. Altan agreed; Zhao and his fellow conspirators were executed, Altan was made Shunyi prince and his capital was renamed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hohhot">Guihua</a>. The American Orientalist Owen Lattimore writes that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> were nominally <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a>, but that the Ming was eager to please them and the arrangement was more or less of benefit to both.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Altan first invited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sonam-gyatso">Sonam Gyatso</a> in 1569; the lama accepted a second invitation in 1577, and Altan later had Thegchen Chonkhor, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a>'s first monastery, built where they met. Sonam Gyatso declared Altan a reincarnation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kublai-khan">Kublai Khan</a>. One reason for adopting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> was a fear among the Mongol elite that the peace of 1571 would spread Chinese cultural influence. Laws set out by Sonam Gyatso and instituted by Altan forbade blood sacrifices and the burning of widows. Altan died on 13 January 1582, aged 74 or 75, and his son Sengge Düüreng succeeded him as Shunyi prince. His great-grandson Yonten Gyatso was chosen as the 4th <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalai-lama">Dalai Lama</a> in 1601.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Altan Khan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1578 <b>Altan Khan</b> gave the Tibetan lama Sonam Gyatso the title of Dalai Lama, and Mongolia turned to Tibetan Buddhism.</blockquote><a href="/essays/faiths-on-the-move">Faiths on the Move</a></li><li><blockquote>Pushed west by Dayan Khan and <b>Altan Khan</b>, the Oirats moved towards Dzungaria in the 16th century and adopted Gelug Buddhism.</blockquote><a href="/state/four-oirat">Four Oirat</a></li><li><blockquote>Dayan Khan reunited the eastern Mongols around 1500 into six tumens, and from the 1570s Tibetan Buddhism spread under <b>Altan Khan</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/northern-yuan">Northern Yuan</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Altan Khan</b>, a grandson of Dayan Khan of the Northern Yuan, became leader of the Tümed tumen in the 1530s and turned it into the strongest power among the eastern Mongols.</blockquote><a href="/state/tumed-altan-khan">Tumed (Altan Khan)</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=46,105&amp;year=1555" aria-label="Mongolia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7032 544 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Altan Khan"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7482 814h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7482 814h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=46,105&amp;year=1555">What was here in 1555? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
